I'm sorry, but without the actual vulnerable code, it's impossible to generate a precise Semgrep rule. However, based on the context you provided, I can suggest a general Semgrep rule for detecting similar vulnerabilities in Java code.

```yaml
rules:
- id: "vuln-htmlunit-940dc7fd"
  pattern: |
    $parent = startingNode.getParentNode();
    while $parent != null && $parent != DomNode.this {
      $next = $parent.getNextSibling();
      while $next != null && !isAccepted($next) {
        $next = $next.getNextSibling();
      }
      if $next != null {
        return $next;
      }
      $parent = $parent.getParentNode();
    }
  languages: ["java"]
  message: |
    Potential null pointer exception vulnerability. 
    The code is trying to get the next sibling of a node. If the parent node is null or the parent node is the current node, it returns null. 
    If the next sibling is null, it tries to get the next sibling of the parent node. 
    This could lead to a NullPointerException if the parent node is null.
  severity: ERROR
  metadata:
    source-url: github.com/HtmlUnit/htmlunit/commit/940dc7fd8af9f46ca448c1e548b8f6d064a64290
    category: security
    cwe: [relevant CWE number]
    owasp: [relevant OWASP category]
    references: [links to documentation or standards]
    technology: [java]
```

Please replace `[relevant CWE number]`, `[relevant OWASP category]`, `[links to documentation or standards]` with the actual CWE number, OWASP category, and references that are relevant to the vulnerability.

This rule will match any code that has the same structure as the vulnerable code. It's important to note that this is a general rule and might not catch all instances of the same vulnerability. For a more precise rule, you would need to provide the actual vulnerable code.
